WEBVTT

00:08:58.177 --> 00:09:00.381
And there were very rich
and tight communities.

00:09:00.405 --> 00:09:03.386
If you grew up in Chicago,
you didn't say, "I'm from Chicago."

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:06.908
You said, "I'm from 59th and Pulaski,"
because that was your neighborhood.

00:09:06.932 --> 00:09:09.036
And that was great,
but it was also stifling.

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:12.929
So in the 1960s, people said,
"I'm being crushed by conformity,

00:09:12.953 --> 00:09:14.938
I'm being crushed by these social roles.

00:09:14.962 --> 00:09:18.357
I can't tolerate the racism
and sexism that is in this culture."

00:09:18.381 --> 00:09:19.538
So they smashed it.

00:09:19.562 --> 00:09:23.774
In 1962, in the Port Huron Statement, say,
a bunch of kids wrote,

00:09:23.798 --> 00:09:25.057
"I'm free to be myself.

00:09:25.081 --> 00:09:28.189
I want individual freedom. I want
to be liberated from restraint."

00:09:28.213 --> 00:09:30.232
And that was important
and necessary to do.